verdict:
ONE FINDING, AND IT IS THE HALF ATTACK POINT (a) DOES NOT MENTION. (b), (c) and (d) resolve, and (c) has a real over-match inside it.

FINDING -- THE READ WAS GIVEN A NAMED MIGRATION REFUSAL AND THE WRITE WAS NOT, AND THE WRITE IS THE MANDATED-REPORTING PATH. Attack point (a) worries that until sql/sairncare_incidents_recorded_by.sql runs the READ answers 503 for every role and the incident log is unreadable. That is true and it is the smaller half. The WRITE sets `incidentRow.recorded_by = session.employee_id` on insert and has NO equivalent guard: on an unmigrated database PostgREST answers 400 undefined_column, the branch falls to `if (!r.ok) return upstream(res, rows)`, and upstream() answers

    502 { error: { message: 'Data store error -- try again' } }

The detail goes to console.error and NOTHING reaches the caller. So the deploy ordering this change chose does not merely make the log unreadable -- IT MAKES FILING AN INCIDENT FAIL WITH A MESSAGE THAT TELLS THE FILER TO RETRY A WRITE THAT CANNOT SUCCEED, on the path the record itself describes as deliberately open to any authenticated employee because of mandated reporting. An abuse_neglect_allegation that a care worker believes they have filed, twice, is a worse outcome than a log nobody can read, and it is the one the 503 was built for and did not get.

THE FIX IS THE SAME SNIFF THE READ ALREADY HAS, moved onto the write's failure path, and it is CHANGE TEXT rather than an edit because api/sd-data.js is inside another session's claim:

    if (!r.ok) {
      let why = null;
      try { why = await r.json(); } catch (_e) { why = null; }
      const msg = JSON.stringify(why || '');
      if (r.status === 400 && /42703|undefined_column/i.test(msg)) {
        res.status(503).json({ error: { code: 'MIGRATION_REQUIRED', message:
          'Incident attribution is not set up yet -- run '
          + 'sql/sairncare_incidents_recorded_by.sql in Supabase. NOTHING WAS '
          + 'RECORDED; this report has not been filed.' } });
        return;
      }
      return upstream(res, rows);
    }

The last sentence matters more than the code: a refusal on a mandated report must say the report did not land, and 'try again' says the opposite.

(a) OTHERWISE THE ORDERING IS THE RIGHT CHOICE AND I WOULD NOT REVERSE IT. Shipping the strip before the migration means the forgeable `reported_by` stops being writable immediately; shipping the migration first would leave a window in which the column exists and the strip does not, which is the same defect with a database change in front of it. Failing closed on an un-migrated read is correct. The gap is that only one of the two paths fails closed LEGIBLY.

(b) RESOLVES. `if (!incBroad) incQ += '&recorded_by=eq.' + session.employee_id` -- a care role gets the filter, a broad role gets none, and `recorded_by=eq.<id>` excludes NULL in PostgREST. So legacy rows are invisible to the care tier and fully visible to management, exactly as claimed, and the decision to leave them NULL is the honest one: back-filling them would invent an attribution.

(c) RESOLVES AS A STRING MATCH, AND IT OVER-MATCHES. The sniff is

    if (/42703|recorded_by|undefined_column/i.test(msg))

on the whole serialised 400 body. `42703` and `undefined_column` are the migration signal. `recorded_by` ALONE IS NOT: any 400 whose body happens to name that column -- a check-constraint violation, a type error on the value, a future NOT NULL -- is reported as MIGRATION_REQUIRED with an instruction to run a migration that is already applied. The record says it never drove this against a real PostgREST 400 and that is the right disclosure; the remedy is to drop `recorded_by` from the alternation and keep the two codes, which are the only two things that mean what the message says. Narrower, and it still catches the case it was built for.

(d) RESOLVES, AND THE MECHANISM IS SOUNDER THAN THE COMMENT CLAIMS. `if (!alreadyExists) incidentRow.recorded_by = ...` omits the key on update, and the request is POST with `Prefer: resolution=merge-duplicates` against `on_conflict=license_hash,entry_id` -- so an omitted column is LEFT ALONE rather than nulled. The original filer survives a management follow-up. Worth stating because the same code with a full-row replace would silently null the attribution on every update, and nothing in the comment distinguishes the two.

(2) alf_family_contacts -- THE BELT-AND-BRACES IS NOT ONE TOO MANY AND THE EMPTY LIST IS THE RIGHT ANSWER. Scoping in the query and again in memory costs one comparison and removes a whole class: a future edit to the query string that drops the filter is caught by the in-memory pass instead of leaking every resident's emergency contacts. That is two independent mechanisms for one guarantee, which is what this platform's own discipline asks for, and the extra alf_clients read per call is a cheap price for it. On the 403-versus-empty question: an empty list is right. A 403 to an unassigned employee tells them a resident exists and that they are not assigned to them, which is information about the roster; an empty list tells them nothing they did not ask for. The consent trail being out of the SELECT rather than filtered after the fetch is also correct -- a field that never leaves the database cannot leak through a logging path.

(3) THE SEVEN UNCHANGED RESOURCES CHECKED OUT AS UNCHANGED. They are named only because api/sd-data-alf-caregiver-scope.test.js drives them as refusal arms, and reading the obligation's file list against the diff confirms it: two branches changed, seven are asserted about.

MEASURED: api/sd-data.js read at HEAD for both branches, the write's failure path traced to upstream() and upstream() read; sql/sairncare_incidents_recorded_by.sql confirmed present in the repo. NOT VERIFIED BY ME: nothing driven against a live database, so whether the migration is applied in production is unknown here and the record's claim that it is not is taken as stated rather than confirmed.
